WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.929
Welcome to SteadierMind.

2
00:00:04.249 --> 00:00:08.237
Anxiety can affect both your thoughts and your body.

3
00:00:08.557 --> 00:00:13.392
One way to understand anxiety is to think of an alarm.

4
00:00:13.712 --> 00:00:21.265
When you anticipate a possible threat or challenge, you may notice worry, a faster

5
00:00:21.265 --> 00:00:23.963
heartbeat, sweating, or tense muscles.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:27.735
Imagine you’re preparing for a presentation.

7
00:00:28.055 --> 00:00:32.406
Your heart races, and you worry about forgetting your words.

8
00:00:32.726 --> 00:00:37.507
Those sensations may reflect your response to the challenge.

9
00:00:37.827 --> 00:00:41.599
They don’t predict how the presentation will go.

10
00:00:41.919 --> 00:00:48.925
If it feels comfortable, notice one thing you can see, or your feet resting against the

11
00:00:48.925 --> 00:00:49.363
floor.

12
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:51.640
You can skip this practice.

13
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:58.015
There’s no need to force a deep breath or make every anxious feeling disappear.

14
00:00:58.335 --> 00:01:05.317
A small next step might be checking your notes, taking a brief pause, or asking for

15
00:01:05.317 --> 00:01:05.753
support.

16
00:01:06.073 --> 00:01:10.461
Physical symptoms can have causes other than anxiety.

17
00:01:10.781 --> 00:01:16.301
Seek appropriate medical advice for new or concerning symptoms.

18
00:01:16.621 --> 00:01:23.564
If anxiety regularly affects your daily life, consider talking with a qualified

19
00:01:23.564 --> 00:01:24.143
professional.

20
00:01:24.463 --> 00:01:31.731
SteadierMind provides general wellness education, not diagnosis or treatment.
